---
components: ["HeatStrip"]
description: Heat strip colors one row of cells on a sequential ramp, to show when a value runs high, such as traffic by hour.
---

<script>
  import { DataTable, Stack } from "carbon-components-svelte";
  import { HeatStrip } from "carbon-components-svelte/viz";

  const byHour = [2, 1, 1, 0, 1, 3, 9, 22, 41, 55, 58, 52, 47, 50, 54, 49, 40, 31, 24, 18, 12, 8, 5, 3];
  const hours = byHour.map((_, hour) => `${String(hour).padStart(2, "0")}:00`);
  const gaps = [4, 6, null, null, 9, 14, 12, 7, null, 3, 5, 8];
</script>

`HeatStrip` is part of the data visualization subpackage. Import it from `carbon-components-svelte/viz` and load `carbon-components-svelte/css/viz.css` alongside `all.css`. With a single-theme stylesheet such as `g100.css`, load the matching `viz-g100.css` instead. The subpackage is experimental: its API may change in a minor release.

## Basic

Pass `values` and a `label`. The range is appended to the accessible name. Add `cellLabels` to say where the peak is.

<HeatStrip values={byHour} cellLabels={hours} label="Traffic by hour" />

## Hue

Set `hue` to `"blue"`, `"purple"`, `"cyan"`, or `"teal"`. The ramp reverses in dark themes, so a high value always stands out from the background.

<Stack gap={5}>
  <HeatStrip values={byHour} hue="purple" label="Purple" />
  <HeatStrip values={byHour} hue="cyan" label="Cyan" />
  <HeatStrip values={byHour} hue="teal" label="Teal" />
</Stack>

## Scale

The ramp spans the smallest to the largest value. Set `min` and `max` to share one scale across several strips.

<Stack gap={5}>
  <HeatStrip values={byHour} min={0} max={60} label="Weekday traffic" />
  <HeatStrip values={byHour.map((v) => Math.round(v / 3))} min={0} max={60} label="Weekend traffic, same scale" />
</Stack>

## Missing values

A missing value is hatched, so it cannot be read as a low one.

<HeatStrip values={gaps} label="Builds per month" />

## Sizes

The width is 192 pixels. Set `style` or a class to change it.

<Stack gap={5}>
  <HeatStrip values={byHour} size="sm" label="Small" />
  <HeatStrip values={byHour} label="Medium (default)" />
  <HeatStrip values={byHour} size="lg" label="Large" />
  <HeatStrip values={byHour} style="inline-size: 100%" label="Full width" />
</Stack>

## In a data table

<DataTable
  size="short"
  headers={[
    { key: "region", value: "Region" },
    { key: "traffic", value: "Traffic by hour (UTC)" },
  ]}
  rows={[
    { id: "a", region: "EMEA", traffic: byHour },
    { id: "b", region: "AMER", traffic: [...byHour.slice(18), ...byHour.slice(0, 18)] },
    { id: "c", region: "APAC", traffic: [...byHour.slice(8), ...byHour.slice(0, 8)] },
  ]}
>
  <svelte:fragment slot="cell" let:cell let:row>
    {#if cell.key === "traffic"}
      <HeatStrip values={cell.value} cellLabels={hours} size="sm" label="{row.region} traffic" />
    {:else}
      {cell.value}
    {/if}
  </svelte:fragment>
</DataTable>

## Accessibility

Color is the only channel a heat strip has, so the accessible name carries the summary: with a `label`, the graphic is announced as an image named with the label, the range, and the peak, such as "Traffic by hour: range 0–58, peak 10:00". Set `translations` to translate the words "range" and "peak". Put exact values in a neighboring cell or a table when they matter. Without a label the strip is hidden from assistive technology.
